=== Bulk arithmetic on float vectors

<procedure>(f32vector-fill! F32VECTOR X [START [END]])</procedure><br>
<procedure>(f64vector-fill! F64VECTOR X [START [END]])</procedure><br>

Stores {{X}} into every element of the vector in the range {{START}} (default
0) up to but not including {{END}} (default the length of the vector).

<procedure>(f32vector-copy! TO AT FROM [START [END]])</procedure><br>
<procedure>(f64vector-copy! TO AT FROM [START [END]])</procedure><br>

Copies the elements of {{FROM}} in the range {{[START,END)}} into {{TO}}
starting at index {{AT}}, exactly as R7RS {{bytevector-copy!}} does for
bytevectors but counting in elements rather than bytes. Overlapping source and
destination ranges of the same vector are handled correctly.

<procedure>(f32vector-scale! F32VECTOR A [START [END]])</procedure><br>
<procedure>(f64vector-scale! F64VECTOR A [START [END]])</procedure><br>

Multiplies every element in the range {{[START,END)}} by {{A}} in place.

<procedure>(f32vector-axpy! Y A X [START [END]])</procedure><br>
<procedure>(f64vector-axpy! Y A X [START [END]])</procedure><br>

Computes {{Y[i] <- A * X[i] + Y[i]}} in place for every {{i}} in the range
{{[START,END)}}. The range applies to both vectors, and both are range
checked: a shorter {{X}} is an error, not a silent truncation.

<procedure>(f32vector-sum F32VECTOR [START [END]])</procedure><br>
<procedure>(f64vector-sum F64VECTOR [START [END]])</procedure><br>

Returns the sum of the elements in the range {{[START,END)}} as a flonum;
{{0.0}} for an empty range.

<procedure>(f32vector-dot X Y [START [END]])</procedure><br>
<procedure>(f64vector-dot X Y [START [END]])</procedure><br>

Returns the inner product of {{X}} and {{Y}} over the range {{[START,END)}} as
a flonum; {{0.0}} for an empty range. As with {{-axpy!}} the range applies to
both vectors and both are range checked.

Notes common to all of these:

* Every operation takes an optional half-open {{[START,END)}} ''element''
range, the same convention used by {{subf64vector}} above and by the R7RS
{{bytevector-copy}} and {{vector-fill!}}.
* The type and range checks are made once per call rather than once per
element, so they are always performed; compiling the caller with {{-unsafe}}
does not remove them.
* {{f32vector}} operations compute in double precision and round on store,
which is what an equivalent loop over {{f32vector-ref}} and
{{f32vector-set!}} does, so the results agree bit for bit.
* {{-fill!}}, {{-copy!}}, {{-scale!}} and {{-axpy!}} produce exactly the same
results as the equivalent element-at-a-time loop. {{-sum}} and {{-dot}} do
not: they accumulate into several independent partial sums which are added
together at the end, so the result is a reassociation of the left-to-right
sum. For values whose partial sums stay in range the difference is a few
units in the last place, and the reassociated form is normally the more
accurate of the two; it is also what allows the loop to be vectorized at all.
When intermediate sums overflow the difference can be qualitative rather than
a matter of ulps -- {{(f64vector-sum (f64vector 1e308 1e308 -1e308 -1e308 0.0
0.0 0.0 0.0))}} is {{+nan.0}} where the left-to-right loop gives {{+inf.0}},
because the independent partial sums overflow to {{+inf.0}} and {{-inf.0}}
and cancel. Code that needs a specific summation order, or that relies on
overflow behaviour, must still write its own loop.

=== Release number vectors allocated in static memory

<procedure>(release-number-vector NVECTOR)</procedure>

Release the storage of a SRFI-4 vector that was allocated in
non-garbage collected memory (for example using the {{NONGC}} argument
for one of the {{make-XXXvector}} constructor procedures). The effect
of calling this procedure with a number vector allocated in normal
garbage collected memory is undefined.

Note that a {{NONGC}} {{u8vector}} is never finalized, whatever the
{{FINALIZE}} argument says: a {{u8vector}} ''is'' its bytevector, so the
whole object lives outside the garbage collected heap, and finalizers can
only be attached to objects inside it. Such a vector must always be
released explicitly. For the other types only the storage is external --
the vector itself is an ordinary garbage collected structure -- so a
finalizer works as documented.


